{
 "id": "060-zipzip-kalbini-duydu",
 "lang": "en",
 "title": "Why Is Hoppy Puffing?",
 "theme": "My body changes when I move",
 "blurb": "Hoppy the rabbit runs and runs across the meadow until he is worn out; his heart goes thump thump and he puffs, huff huff. He thinks he is ill. When he rests with his friends and tries his own experiment, he discovers his body's secret.",
 "voiceNames": {
  "zipzip": "Hoppy (rabbit)",
  "tosbi": "Shelly (tortoise)",
  "diken": "Spike (hedgehog)"
 },
 "pages": [
  {
   "text": "It was a spring afternoon. Hoppy the rabbit started running across the meadow. “Hop, hop, hop! Catch me!” he shouted. He bounced between the flowers and over the stones. Spike the hedgehog and Shelly the tortoise were left behind. “Wait for us, Hoppy!” they called.",
   "hits": {
    "zipzip": {
     "say": "Hop, hop! Catch me!"
    },
    "diken": {
     "say": "Wait for us, Hoppy!"
    },
    "tosbi": {
     "say": "Step, step, I'm coming!"
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "say": "Hop!",
    "done": "Hoppy reached the other end of the meadow!",
    "msg": "Hop hop, he made it!"
   },
   "hint": "Touch Hoppy so he runs!",
   "ask": "Do you like running? Where do you run the most?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Suddenly Hoppy stopped. His heart was going thump thump thump. He was breathing fast: huff, huff, huff! His long ears had gone all hot. Hoppy got scared. “My heart is so fast! Am I ill?”",
   "hits": {
    "kalp": {
     "say": "Thump thump thump!"
    },
    "zipzip": {
     "say": "Huff, huff… My heart is so fast!"
    },
    "diken": {
     "say": "We're here, we're here!"
    },
    "tosbi": {
     "say": "Nearly there, Hoppy!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch the heart and listen!",
   "ask": "What changed in Hoppy's body? What happens in your body when you run?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Spike caught up, all out of breath. Shelly came slowly too: step, step, one more step. Hoppy's ears drooped: “I'm so tired. My heart's going thump thump. Am I ill?” Shelly smiled: “Let's rest first, then we'll look together.”",
   "hits": {
    "zipzip": {
     "say": "I'm so tired. Am I ill?"
    },
    "tosbi": {
     "say": "Let's rest first."
    },
    "diken": {
     "say": "Huff, huff… I'm tired too!"
    }
   },
   "predict": {
    "q": "What do you think will slow Hoppy's heart down?",
    "res": "Both of them work! Let's see.",
    "opts": [
     {
      "l": "Resting in the shade"
     },
     {
      "l": "Breathing slowly"
     }
    ]
   },
   "hint": "Touch Hoppy!",
   "ask": "What did Hoppy say when he got tired? What do you say when you're tired?"
  },
  {
   "text": "They moved into the tree's cool shade. Shelly pointed to the flowers: “One breath for every flower. Smell through your nose, blow slowly through your mouth.” One… two… three… four… five… Hoppy's heart slowed down.",
   "hits": {
    "c": {
     "say": "Mmmm… fwoooo…"
    },
    "zipzip": {
     "say": "My heart is slowing down."
    },
    "tosbi": {
     "say": "Slowly, slowly. No rush."
    },
    "diken": {
     "say": "I'm counting too!"
    },
    "kh": {
     "say": "Fwoooo… The seeds are flying!"
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "done": "Five slow breaths! His heart is going pit, pat.",
    "msg": "Five breaths!"
   },
   "hint": "One slow breath for every flower!",
   "ask": "Now you take five flower breaths. Then listen to your heart: how is it beating?"
  },
  {
   "text": "Hoppy had an idea: “Let me try something!” He ran to the log: hop, hop, hop! His heart went thump thump again. Spike ran too: “Mine's going thump thump as well!” Hoppy laughed: “So I'm not ill. Running makes your heart go faster!”",
   "hits": {
    "zipzip": {
     "say": "So I'm not ill!"
    },
    "kalp": {
     "say": "Thump thump thump!"
    },
    "diken": {
     "say": "Mine's going thump thump too!"
    },
    "tosbi": {
     "say": "I'm coming too, slowly, slowly!"
    }
   },
   "mech": {
    "say": "Hop!",
    "done": "Hoppy reached the log. His heart is going thump thump!",
    "msg": "Hop hop, he made it!"
   },
   "hint": "Touch Hoppy so he runs!",
   "ask": "You jump ten times on the spot too. Then put your hand on your chest. What did you feel?"
  },
  {
   "text": "At sunset, the three friends rested side by side. Hoppy put a paw on his heart: pit, pat, pit. His breathing was slower too. “I get it! Running makes my heart faster and my breath quicker. Resting makes them both slower.” He pricked up his ears: “Let's run again tomorrow. And rest when we're tired!”",
   "hits": {
    "zipzip": {
     "say": "When I'm tired, I rest!"
    },
    "kalp": {
     "say": "Pit, pat, pit."
    },
    "tosbi": {
     "say": "Let's run again tomorrow!"
    },
    "diken": {
     "say": "I'm coming too!"
    }
   },
   "hint": "Touch the heart and listen!",
   "ask": "When do you need a rest? Who do you tell?"
  }
 ],
 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "HSAB.8.a — Notices changes in breathing, heartbeat and sweating during movement: notices Hoppy's thumping heart, his huffing breath and his hot ears; and in their own body, a faster heart, quicker breathing and sweating.",
   "HSAB.8.c — Expresses the need to rest when necessary: says what they need, like Hoppy: “I'm so tired, I need a little rest.”",
   "HSAB.9 — Moving for an active and healthy life: enjoys running and jumping; explains that movement and rest together are healthy.",
   "HSAB.1.a — Performs locomotor movements in different settings and conditions: runs, jumps, walks slowly and quickly.",
   "SDB1.1 Self-Knowledge — Recognises the signs in their body (fast heart, quick breathing); instead of being scared, tries to understand what is happening.",
   "SDB1.2 Self-Regulation — Calms their body by breathing slowly and resting; uses the “five slow breaths” method themselves.",
   "D13 Healthy Living — Says that moving, and resting when tired, are good for their body."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Point to Hoppy on the cover: ask “How do rabbits move? How do you run your fastest?”",
   "Let the children put their hands on their chests and quietly listen to their hearts: “How is it beating right now?”"
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 1, touch Hoppy to make him run; the children can run on the spot with little steps too.",
   "On page 2, touch the heart: “Why did Hoppy get scared? Do you think he's really ill?” For the prediction question on page 3, accept both answers.",
   "On page 4, before touching each flower, wait for one full breath together: smell through the nose, blow slowly through the mouth. Count to five without hurrying.",
   "On page 5, do Hoppy's experiment yourselves: jump ten times on the spot, then put your hand on your chest."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "What changed in Hoppy's body when he ran?",
   "Why did Hoppy think he was ill? What did he learn afterwards?",
   "What happened to Hoppy's heart when he rested?",
   "How can you tell you're tired? Who do you tell, and how?"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Heart Detectives",
   "sure": "20 min",
   "materyal": "Paper heart cards (big: fast, small: slow), music, five flower pictures",
   "adim": [
    "The children sit quietly with their hands on their chests and listen to their hearts; the teacher taps the “pit, pat” rhythm on the board.",
    "When the music starts, they run and jump on the spot for one minute. Children who cannot run on the spot can join in by swinging their arms quickly. When the music stops, they put their hands on their chests and hold up the big or the small heart card; talk about their breathing and sweating.",
    "Looking at the five flower pictures, take five slow breaths; listen to the heart again and ask “What happened when you rested?” Each child says the sentence “I'm tired, I need a rest.” in their own words."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "Why did Hoppy get scared?",
   "What experiment did Hoppy do?",
   "How does your heart beat when you run? What happens when you rest?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Heart Listening at Home",
   "d": "First sit down together, put your hand on your chest and listen to your heart. Then run and jump in the hallway or the garden for a while and listen again. Finally, take five slow breaths and listen once more. Describe the difference together as “pit pat” or “thump thump”."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “Why Is Hoppy Puffing?” When Hoppy the rabbit runs a lot, he notices that his heart speeds up and his breathing gets quicker, and at first he thinks he is ill. When he rests, breathes slowly and does his own experiment, he learns that this is a natural result of moving. During play at home, you can ask your child “How is your heart beating?” and support them in resting when they say they are tired. If your child says their heart is racing, they feel dizzy or they find it hard to breathe even without running, take it seriously and consult a health professional."
 },
